Volume = {84},
Month = {SEP},
Abstract = {The severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2 is currently
   spreading across the world at an alarming rate, resulting in the
   coronavirus disease 2019 pandemic. Amidst this crisis, tourism scholars
   are directing their attention to communities at tourist destinations,
   looking at their safety and well-being and the costs that they will bear
   due to the cessation of tourism activity. This article describes
   residents' perceptions of the risks posed by tourism activity, and
   estimates their willingness to pay to reduce public health risks based
   on hypothetical scenarios, using the triple-bounded dichotomous choice
   contingent valuation method. The social costs in three urban
   destinations are assessed and compared. Based on the findings,
   suggestions are made for appropriate post-pandemic recovery actions by
   local authorities and tourism organizations.},
